DRAWTERM IN A TERMINAL: THE KITTY BACKEND
=========================================

CONF=kitty builds a drawterm that opens no window.  It takes over
the terminal emulator it is started in and draws the Plan 9 screen
there with the kitty graphics protocol, reading the keyboard and
mouse through the kitty keyboard protocol and SGR pixel mouse
reports.  It links against nothing but libc, libpthread and zlib,
so one binary works over ssh, on a console-only machine, or on a
Mac without XQuartz.

Ghostty is the reference terminal and the one the code is tuned
for; kitty works as well.  Usage is in drawterm(1): see the DTKITTY
variable and the KITTY TERMINALS section.  This file describes how
the backend works and what is left to do.


FILES
-----
Make.kitty	build configuration: GUI=kitty, AUDIO=none,
		links -lz (and -lrt on Linux for shm_open)
gui-kitty/kitty.c	the whole backend, about 2100 lines
kern/devdraw.c	one general fix (see DAMAGE below)
drawterm.1	DTKITTY options, KITTY TERMINALS


STRUCTURE
---------
The backend implements the usual drawterm screen interface
(kern/screen.h): screeninit, screensize, flushmemscreen,
attachscreen, setcursor, mouseset, getcolor/setcolor, clipread,
clipwrite and guimain.  gscreen is a soft screen in XRGB32 that
devdraw draws into; flushmemscreen only records damage.

Two kprocs do the work:

  kittyout (outproc)	the only writer to the terminal.  It
		wakes on damage, cursor changes or pending escape
		sequences, copies the damaged pixels out under
		drawlock, encodes them and writes one frame.
  kittyin (inproc)	the only reader.  It parses everything
		the terminal sends (keys, mouse, replies, resize
		reports, pastes) and feeds kbdkey and
		absmousetrack.

Shared output state (damage list, cursor, pending sequences,
flow-control counters, clipboard) lives in one struct under a
pthread mutex with a condition variable; the writer sleeps on it.


STARTUP
-------
1. Options are read from $DTKITTY.

2. stdout and stderr, if they are the terminal, are redirected to
   an unlinked temporary file so stray prints cannot scribble over
   the picture.  The last 8KB are shown on stderr when drawterm
   exits.

3. /dev/tty is put in raw mode and these modes are set:

	CSI ?1049h		alternate screen
	CSI ?25l		no text cursor
	CSI 22;2t		save the window title
	CSI ?1000h ?1002h ?1003h	all mouse motion
	CSI ?1006h ?1016h	SGR mouse reports, in pixels
	CSI ?1004h		focus events
	CSI ?2004h		bracketed paste
	CSI ?2048h		in-band resize reports

   An atexit handler and handlers for SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGQUIT and
   SIGHUP undo all of it (async-signal-safe), pop the keyboard
   flags, delete our images and unlink any shared memory objects
   left behind.

4. The terminal is probed in one write, and its answers are read
   until the primary device attributes come back (terminals answer
   in order, so DA1 marks the end; 5 s timeout):

	a=q, t=d		kitty graphics at all
	a=q, t=s		can it open our POSIX shared memory
	a=t, a=f, a=d		can it edit frames in place (a=f)
	CSI ?u			kitty keyboard protocol
	CSI ?2026$p ?1016$p ?2048$p	synchronized output, pixel
				mouse, in-band resize (DECRQM)
	CSI 14t, CSI 16t	text area and cell size in pixels
	CSI >q			name and version (XTVERSION)
	CSI 5n			does it answer status reports
	CSI c			DA1, the end marker

   Without graphics drawterm refuses to start (inside tmux or
   screen, for example).  With the keyboard protocol it pushes
   flags 31 (CSI >31u: disambiguate, report event types, report
   alternate keys, report all keys as escapes, report associated
   text).  The title is set to "drawterm".

5. The size is taken (see RESIZING), gscreen is allocated, the two
   kprocs start and the Plan 9 console is drawn.


OUTPUT
------
Tiles.  The screen is a grid of tiles, each a separate image
placed once.  A tile is a whole number of cells, at least
tile=n pixels (256 by default) on a side; on a 10x19 cell grid
that is 260x266.  If the grid would have more than 1024 tiles the
size doubles until it does not.  Tiles at the right and bottom
edges are clipped to the screen.  tile=0 makes the whole screen
one image.

Why tiles: Ghostty (1.3 and 1.4) and kitty upload a changed image
to the GPU whole.  Ghostty copies the image, converts it and
creates a new texture on every content change.  With the screen
as one image a keystroke cost a full-screen upload: 4MB at
1280x800, 16MB at 2560x1600, about 59MB at 5K.  With tiles only
the touched tiles are re-uploaded.

Frames.  A full update (start, resize, error recovery) deletes all
our images (a=d,d=A) and transmits and places every tile:

	CSI row;col H
	APC G a=T,i=<tile>,p=1,s=w,v=h,X=dx,Y=dy,C=1,z=0,f=24,q=1;...

C=1 keeps the terminal cursor still; z=0 puts the screen under
the cursor image.  After that, when the terminal can edit frames,
damage within each tile is sent as an edit of the tile's root
frame, which leaves the placement alone:

	APC G a=f,i=<tile>,r=1,x=..,y=..,s=..,v=..,X=1,f=24,q=1;...

Terminals without a=f (Ghostty 1.3) get every damaged tile
retransmitted whole with a=T, which replaces the image and its
placement in one command.

Pixels.  gscreen is XRGB32; damage is converted to packed RGB
(f=24) while drawlock is held, then encoded without it.  Pixels
travel one of two ways:

  shared memory	t=s: each payload is a new POSIX shm object
		"/dtk<pid>.<seq>", which the terminal maps and
		unlinks.  Only the name crosses the tty: a
		keystroke is about 110 bytes.  Used when the probe
		shows the terminal can open it, i.e. same machine.
		A ring of the last 4096 names is kept so anything the
		terminal did not unlink is removed on exit.
  inline	t=d: zlib (level 1, o=z, only if it shrinks the
		data) then base64, in 4096-byte chunks (m=1).
		Used over ssh.

All commands carry q=1, so the terminal stays quiet except for
errors.

Frames are paced:

  - at most fps=n frames a second (60); damage arriving meanwhile
    accumulates.
  - each frame is wrapped in synchronized output (CSI ?2026h ...
    ?2026l) when the terminal has it, so it never shows half a
    frame.
  - each frame is followed by CSI 5n, and the writer keeps at most
    one frame in flight: it waits for the status report of the
    previous one before writing the next.  A slow terminal (or
    link) therefore gets fewer, larger frames instead of a growing
    backlog.  If no answer comes in 3 s flow control is turned off.

Errors.  Graphics errors are logged.  ENOENT and ENOPARENT mean
the terminal lost our images (a reset, or a terminal that evicts
images under memory pressure); drawterm then resends everything,
at most once a second.

Image ids start at 0x44540000 ("DT") so they do not clash with
anybody else's.


DAMAGE
------
flushmemscreen adds its rectangle to a list of up to 32; a new
rectangle is merged with an old one when the union wastes less
than 32x32 pixels, and when the list is full the pair whose union
grows least is merged.  The writer then cuts each rectangle at
tile boundaries and keeps one bounding box per tile.

kern/devdraw.c (applies to every drawterm backend): for a draw
message devdraw used to report the whole destination rectangle as
damage, but programs often pass r = screen->r and let a small
source clip it (catclock draws its eyes and tail this way), so
every tick damaged the whole screen.  devdraw now clips r the way
memdraw does (drawclipnorepl) and reports only what was drawn.
catclock went from all 15 tiles a tick to 2.


THE PLAN 9 CURSOR
-----------------
The terminal's pointer cannot be changed to an arbitrary bitmap,
so the Plan 9 cursor is drawn as a 16x16 RGBA image (f=32, z=1,
above the screen) placed at the mouse position and moved by
placing it again (a=p with x,y,w,h cropping at the edges and X,Y
for the offset within a cell).  setcursor retransmits the image.
mouseset cannot warp the host pointer, so it only moves the drawn
cursor.

cursor=n: 0 never draws it, 1 always, 2 (the default) not while
it is the arrow, since the terminal's own pointer is a fine arrow.
Other cursors (rio's sweep cross, the menu box, programs' own) are
drawn, with the host pointer still visible on top: see TODO.


KEYBOARD
--------
With the kitty keyboard protocol every key reports press, repeat
and release, including bare modifiers.  These go to kbdkey as
Plan 9 runes, i.e. to /dev/kbd as 'r'/'R' messages, so kbdfs sees
real key ups and downs: Alt alone starts a compose sequence as on
a Plan 9 terminal, and Shift, Ctl and Mod4 are tracked by kbdfs.

Mapping (CSI code[:shifted[:base]];mods[:event];text u, and the
legacy-shaped CSI 1;mods A..S and CSI n;mods ~):

  - The associated text, when there is some and Ctl is not held,
    is used as the rune.  This gives the layout's characters, dead
    keys and IME output.
  - Otherwise the code: Escape, Enter, Tab, Backspace map to Kesc,
    '\n', '\t', Kbs; arrows, Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Ins, Del, F1-F12
    to the Plan 9 keys; keypad keys to their characters or
    movement keys; Shift, Ctl, Alt, Super (left and right) to
    Kshift, Kctl, Kalt, Kmod4; ISO level 3 shift to Kaltgr; media
    keys to Kpause, Ksfwd, Ksbwd, Kvoldn, Kvolup, Kmute.  F13 and
    up, Hyper, Meta and Caps Lock are dropped.
  - With Shift and no text, the shifted alternate is used; with
    Ctl, ASCII runes become control characters (r & 0x1f).
  - Each held key is remembered by its physical id, so its release
    sends the rune its press sent even if the modifiers changed in
    between (press a, press Shift, release a).
  - On focus loss everything held is released, so a key that went
    up in another window does not stay down; if Alt was held a
    press and release of Alt follows, as gui-x11 does.

Terminals without the protocol (and the probe window before it is
enabled) fall back to legacy decoding: UTF-8 bytes are typed whole
(down and up), CR is '\n', DEL is Kbs, CSI/SS3 arrows and tilde keys
are mapped, and a lone ESC becomes Kesc after 30 ms.  No releases
and no bare modifiers, so no Alt compose.

Bracketed paste is typed rune by rune; CRLF becomes '\n' and other
control characters are dropped.


MOUSE
-----
Modes 1003, 1006 and 1016 give SGR reports with coordinates in
pixels for every motion.  Buttons 1-3 map to Plan 9 buttons 1, 2
and 4, the vertical wheel to 8 and 16 (a press and a release), and
each terminal button remembers which Plan 9 button its press sent
so the release matches.  The horizontal wheel, buttons 8 and up
and kitty's "left the window" report are ignored.  Without 1016
cell coordinates are used, aimed at the middle of the cell.

emu: Alt-click is button 2 and Ctl-click is button 3, for one-
button pointing devices; the Alt release of an Alt-click is
followed by an Alt press and release so it does not leave a
compose sequence pending.


RESIZING
--------
The Plan 9 screen is the terminal's text area rounded down to
whole cells, because placements are addressed by cell.  The size
comes from, in order of preference:

  - in-band reports (mode 2048, CSI 48;rows;cols;hpx;wpx t),
    which are exact and arrive in order with everything else; when
    the terminal has them SIGWINCH is ignored.
  - SIGWINCH and TIOCGWINSZ, using its pixel size when set,
    otherwise the cell size from CSI 16t, otherwise the text area
    from CSI 14t.

A new pixel size goes through screenresize (rio and the console
follow).  A new cell size with the same pixel size only moves
placements and triggers a full resend.


CLIPBOARD
---------
clipwrite (Plan 9 snarf) is sent to the host clipboard with
OSC 52 and kept locally.  clipread returns the local copy; with
the clipread option it asks the terminal with OSC 52 ;c;? first
and waits up to 2 s, because terminals usually prompt or refuse
clipboard reads.  Pasting with the terminal's own paste key always
works: it arrives as a bracketed paste and is typed.


PANICS AND EXIT
---------------
The kernel hangs after a panic to keep the message on the screen.
The reader watches for that: the end of a session comes as
panic("ending"), on which drawterm restores the terminal and
exits; any other panic is put in the window title and the next
key restores the terminal and exits with the message on stderr.
If the terminal goes away (hangup, EOF) drawterm exits.


DEBUGGING
---------
DTKITTY=debug=/tmp/dt.log logs the probe results, sizes, every
frame (full or not, tiles sent, bytes), key ups and downs and
graphics errors, with timestamps in ms.  For example:

  terminal 'ghostty 1.4.0': da=1 gfx=1 shm=1 frames=1 kbd=1
      pixels=1 sync=1 inband=1 ack=1 cell=10x19 text=1280x798
  size 128x42 cells of 10x19
  frame full=0 frames=1 tiles=1/15 cursor=0/0 bytes=110

shm, noshm, frames, noframes, tile=n, fps=n, z=n and cursor=n
force the choices the probe would make, which helps to tell a
terminal bug from ours.


MEASUREMENTS
------------
On a 2-CPU VM against a 9front cpu server, Ghostty 1.4.0 under
Xvfb with software GL (llvmpipe):

  - a keystroke at the console: 1 of 15 tiles, about 110 bytes
    through the tty with shared memory.
  - catclock at 2560x1596, 5 fps: Ghostty's renderer thread
    (image copy before upload) spent 71-80 ms/s with one screen
    image and 12-40 ms/s with tiles.  Software rasterization
    (about 1.5 CPUs either way) dominated the totals, so these
    numbers are only indicative of what a GPU machine saves.
  - rotzoomer (the whole screen changes every frame) at 1280x798:
    about 21 fps whichever the mode; tiles neither help nor hurt
    when everything changes.


TESTED
------
  Ghostty 1.4.0 (Linux, X11)	frames, shm, everything above
  Ghostty 1.3.1 (Linux, X11)	no a=f: tiles sent whole
  kitty 0.49.2 (Linux, X11)	frames, shm
  libghostty-vt at v1.4.0	headless, for scripted tests

Under test were the console, rio (menus, sweeping windows,
resizing), catclock and rotzoomer, typing and compose, the
clipboard, terminal resizes and session end.  The macOS arm64
binary is built in CI but has not been run in Ghostty on a Mac by
us.  WezTerm, Konsole, iTerm2 and others are untested.


KNOWN PROBLEMS (NOT IN THIS BACKEND)
------------------------------------
  - At the bare console (no rio), keys typed faster than the
    console echoes them can be lost.  The remote aux/kbdfs drops
    runes it cannot hand over at once (nbsend on an unbuffered
    channel); under rio they go through a buffered channel and
    nothing is lost.  The same happens with other backends.
  - drawterm's exit status at the end of a session is taken from
    the first character of the remote status (upstream rcpu).


TODO
----
Roughly in order of value for Ghostty users.

  - HiDPI.  Terminals report device pixels, so on a Retina screen
    the Plan 9 screen has twice the pixels a window would and its
    fonts look small.  A scale=2 option should draw gscreen at
    half size and double the pixels in drawterm: letting the
    terminal scale the image (placing it over c,r cells) blurs it,
    because Ghostty samples images linearly.  Mouse coordinates
    divide by the same factor.

  - Hide the host pointer while a Plan 9 cursor other than the
    arrow is drawn.  Today both show.  Ghostty 1.4 has no way to
    hide it from an application (OSC 22 has no blank shape there);
    this needs a terminal feature, or OSC 22 shapes that
    approximate Plan 9's common cursors.

  - Upstream: the backend and the devdraw fix are not yet offered
    to 9front.  Ghostty could also update textures in place
    (glTexSubImage2D or the Metal equivalent) on a=f; then the
    single-image path would be as cheap as tiles and could become
    the default again.

  - Skip unchanged pixels.  rio and libdraw often redraw what is
    already there.  Keeping a copy of what was sent and shrinking
    each tile's box to the pixels that differ would cut traffic
    over ssh, where every byte is base64 over the link.

  - Scrolling.  A scroll arrives as damage over the whole text
    area and is resent.  Copying within the terminal (a=c
    composition) could do it, but devdraw does not tell the
    backend a draw was a copy, and Ghostty rejects a=c with
    overlapping source and destination in one image, so scrolls
    would have to be done tile to tile.

  - Better compression for remote use: PNG (f=100) or a faster
    codec choice per tile, measured against zlib level 1.

  - tmux and screen: drawterm refuses to run inside them.  The
    kitty protocol's Unicode placeholders (U=1) with tmux
    passthrough could make it work there.

  - Clipboard reads without a prompt: look at the kitty clipboard
    protocol (OSC 5522), which Ghostty has started to implement.

  - Input left out: the horizontal wheel, mouse buttons 8 and up,
    F13 and up (Plan 9 has no keys for them).  Keys the terminal
    binds for itself (copy, paste, new tab, ...) never reach
    drawterm and must be unbound in the terminal's configuration;
    shift-click is usually kept by the terminal for selection.

  - Audio: built with AUDIO=none, so there is no /dev/audio.  One
    of the existing posix audio backends could be linked in, at
    the cost of a dependency.

  - Window label: drawterm has no /dev/label; the title stays
    "drawterm".  Setting it from rio's label would need one.

  - Other terminals: try WezTerm, Konsole, iTerm2 and others, and
    adjust the probe for what they get wrong.

  - Windows (ConPTY) is not supported: the backend is POSIX
    (termios, shm_open, select).
